\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, Q: d1-c2, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-d3, Q: d1-e2, P: d5-c6, P: d5-d6, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, N: f3-d2, N: f3-e5, N: f3-g1, N: f3-g5, N: f3-h4, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in an early-mid game state. My king is safe on e1, and both kings have not moved, so castling rights are intact. Black has moved their queen to d7 (QBF1), which is somewhat exposed, and a black pawn is on c3 (PBF1), deep in my territory but not immediately threatening. Two white pawns on d4 and d5 (PWF1) are advanced and can potentially support a push for promotion. The knight on f3 is well-placed, eyeing central squares like e5 and g5. Black’s king is still in the center (e8), and they haven’t castled yet either.\n\nBlack has a bishop on c8 (BBF1) that may be preparing to develop, and their queenside is weakened with missing pawns on c7 and d7. However, their rooks are still connected on the back rank. My light-squared bishop on c1 is blocked in by pawns, and needs a way out. The pawn on b2 is also a bit awkward, blocking the bishop on c1.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: d5-d6 – Pushing the d-pawn could gain space and open lines for my queen or bishop. It attacks e7 and could threaten the black queen if it were on e7, but currently QBF1 is on d7. This move might be met with exd6, opening the e-file.\n- P: a2-a4 – A waiting move, gaining space on the queenside, possibly preparing b4 later. Low immediate impact.\n- N: f3-e5 – Not listed, so not legal? Wait — valid moves include N: f3-e5? No, it\'s not in the list! So not available.\nWait — checking valid moves again: N: f3-e5 **is** in the list. Yes! “N: f3-e5” is a valid move.\n\nWait — correction: yes, "N: f3-e5" **is** in the list.\n\nSo re-evaluating:\n- N: f3-e5 – Strong candidate. Central outpost, attacks key squares like d7 (black queen), f7 (weak point, only defended by king), and c6. If black plays ...f6, we can retreat. But this knight becomes very active.\n- B: c1-g5 – Not directly possible due to pawn on d4 and e5 being blocked? Wait — path is c1-d2-e3-f4-g5? That would require moving through multiple pieces. Currently, d4 has a white pawn (PWF1), e4 is empty? Wait no — e4 is empty? Let\'s see:\n\nBoard:\n- e4: empty\n- f4: empty\n- g5: empty\n\nBut from c1 to g5: c1-d2-e3-f4-g5 — diagonal. But d2 is empty? Yes, d2 is empty. So B: c1-g5 is possible? But wait — is it in the valid move list?\n\nNo — valid moves list includes: B: c1-h6, B: c1-g5 is NOT listed. Only up to h6 via long diagonal? But c1 to h6 is a diagonal? c1-d2-e3-f4-g5-h6 — yes, same diagonal.\n\nBut “B: c1-h6” is listed — meaning the bishop can go all the way to h6. So intermediate square g5 is reachable. But why isn\'t B: c1-g5 listed? Wait — actually, looking at the list: “B: c1-h6” is listed, but not g5. That suggests the engine-generated valid moves only show *some* options? Or perhaps I misread.\n\nWait — no, the list says: B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6 — YES! “B: c1-g5” IS in the list!\n\nI missed it earlier.\n\nSo:\n- B: c1-g5 – Attacks g5, pins knight on f6? But there is no black knight on f6. Black has nothing on f6. But g5 is near black’s kingside. Could prepare kingside attack. But black can play ...h6 to kick it. Also, bishop might be trapped after ...h6 and ...g5.\n\nBut more importantly: **N: f3-e5** is excellent.\n\n- N: f3-e5 – Knight jumps to e5, attacking d7 (queen), f7 (only guarded by king), and supporting potential d6 break.\nIf Black plays ...Qd8, we can follow up with d6, opening lines.\nEven better: if Black doesn’t respond, we threaten Nxg7+ (forking king and rook after capturing on g7), because h8 rook is undefended!\n\nWait — Nxg7+? From e5 to g7 is not a knight move. e5 to g6 or f7 — yes. e5 to g7? No — that’s two files over and two ranks — not an L-shape.\n\nKnight on e5 attacks:\n- f7 (yes — one forward, two right)\n- d7 (one forward, two left)\n- g6, g4, f3, d3, c4, c6\n\nSo f7 is attacked — and f7 is only defended by the black king. f7 is a classic weak point.\n\nSo **N: f3-e5** puts pressure on f7.\n\nAdditionally, if black plays ...f6, we can play Nxd7, winning the queen.\n\nBut even better: after Ne5, if black ignores it, we can consider Qh5, threatening mate on f7.\n\nBut for now, Ne5 is strong.\n\nOther options:\n- P: d5-d6 – Also good. Challenges the center, opens diagonals. After d6, if ...exd6, then e5 can be occupied by knight or piece. But d6 might be met with ...cxd6, keeping tension.\nBut d6 weakens e6, and if black plays ...e5, they gain space.\n\nBut Ne5 avoids committal pawn moves.\n\nAlso, developing a piece (knight to e5) is better than a pawn move when you can seize the initiative.\n\nCompare to:\n- Q: d1-d3 – Supports e4 or f3, but not urgent.\n- B: c1-g5 – Could be useful, but black can play ...h6 and ...g5, kicking it. And we lose tempo.\n\nWhereas **N: f3-e5** cannot be easily removed — only by ...f6 or ...Nxe5, but ...Nxe5 loses time unless recaptured.\n\nAnd crucially: **after Ne5, we threaten Nxf7**, forking king and rook! Because after Nxf7, Kxf7, Qxh7 wins the rook on h8.\n\nYes! **Nxf7** is a tactical shot.\n\nSequence:\n1. Ne5 → threatens Nxf7\n2. If Kxf7, then Qxh7+ Ke6 (only) 3. Qxg7 — winning a rook and pawn for knight — material gain and dangerous attack.\n\nEven if Black plays ...Kf8 before, we still have a strong knight on e5.\n\nSo **N: f3-e5** is a powerful move, creating immediate threats.\n\nIs it safe? Can Black capture on f3? No — no piece attacks f3. So yes.\n\nTherefore, best move is **N: f3-e5**\n\nAnswer: N: f3-e5'}
