\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, Q: d1-c2, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-d3, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, P: d5-c6, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, N: e5-c4, N: e5-c6, N: e5-d3, N: e5-d7, N: e5-f3, N: e5-f7, N: e5-g4, N: e5-g6, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in an early-mid game state. Several of my pieces have developed, notably the knight on e5 (NWF1), which is centrally placed and active. Black has weakened their kingside structure slightly with a missing g7 pawn and somewhat exposed king, though castling has likely already occurred (black king on e8, rooks on h8 and a8 suggest standard setup). My central pawns on d4 and e5 exert influence, but there are also opportunities to develop more pieces or increase pressure.\n\nKey observations:\n- The knight on e5 is already well-placed and attacking key central squares.\n- My light-squared bishop on c1 is blocked in by pawns; moving it via Bc1-d2/e3/f4/g5/h6 could open lines.\n- The queen on d1 can support development or central control.\n- Pawns on d4 and e5 form a strong center, but the d4 pawn is under no immediate threat.\n- Castling hasn\'t been done yet for me — king is still on e1, rooks on corners. This suggests I should consider king safety soon.\n- The black queen on d6 is somewhat exposed; targeting it with development could be useful.\n\nPromising moves:\n1. N: e5-c6 — attacks the black queen directly and threatens material gain if not defended properly. However, this may allow ...Qxd4 or counterplay.\n2. B: c1-g5 — develops the bishop actively, pins the knight on f6 if Black had one (but current position shows no knight on f6), however in this position Black doesn’t have a knight on f6 (f7 pawn is gone), so less effective. Still, it pressures h6 and eyes g7.\n3. Q: d1-h5 — aggressive queen move threatening checkmate on h7 if not careful, but possibly premature without sufficient backup.\n4. N: b1-c3 — develops a knight toward the center, supports d5 push later, natural developing move.\n5. P: a2-a4 or P: b2-b4 — flank pawn moves, less urgent than development or threats.\n6. K: e1-e2? — too risky without necessity; blocks queen and bishop lines.\n7. N: e5-f7+ — possible fork idea? But f7 is protected by g7 pawn? Wait — no g7 pawn! Black’s g7 pawn is missing — only h7, f7 pawns present. Let\'s verify: Black has pawns on a7, b7, e7, f7, g7?, wait — in row 7: a7, b7, e7, f7, g7, h7 — yes, g7 is occupied. So f7 is protected. Nxf7 would be met with Kxf7, losing the knight without clear compensation.\n\nBut N: e5-c6 stands out — attacks the black queen on d6, which must move or be defended. After Nc6, if ...Qd7, then I can play Bf4 or Qe2, gaining tempo and initiative.\n\nWait — valid move list includes **N: e5-c6** — yes, available.\n\nAlso, **B: c1-g5** is available — puts pressure on the kingside, potentially preparing Bh6 or exchange.\n\nBut let’s evaluate **N: e5-c6** more closely:\n- Attacks black queen on d6.\n- If Black plays ...Qd7, I recapture with bxc6? Not necessarily — I don’t have a pawn on b-file near c6. No, after Nc6, if queen moves, I just win time — no immediate capture unless I want to double pawns.\n- But material-wise, it’s just a threat to trade knight for queen — they’ll move the queen.\n- However, after ...Qe7 or ...Qb6, I might follow up with Nb5 or Na4, eyeing weaknesses.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: d1-h5** is very sharp:\n- Threatens Qxh7# if not stopped.\n- Black must respond — likely ...g6 or ...Qe7.\n- After ...g6, Qh4, then I have strong attack brewing, especially if I can bring up bishops or knights.\n- But riskier — exposes queen.\n\nHowever, looking at the board: black king is on e8, likely castled queenside? Or not yet? Rooks are on a8 and h8, king on e8 — so either not castled, or castled queenside (which would place king on c8). Since king is on e8, probably **not castled yet**.\n\nWait — black king is on e8 (KBF0), and hasn\'t moved (flag is F0 → never moved). Same with rooks. So **Black has not castled**.\n\nThis is critical.\n\nSo king is still in center — didn\'t castle.\n\nTherefore, **Q: d1-h5** is extremely dangerous for Black.\n\nAfter Qh5, threat is Qxh7#. Only defenses are:\n- ...g6 — blocking, but then Qh4, and I can follow with Bg5, Nf3, etc., massive attack.\n- ...Qe7 — blocks, but loses queen after Nxg7+\nWait — N: e5-g6!? Not in move list — e5 to g6 is listed: **N: e5-g6** is in the valid moves!\n\nWait — yes: "N: e5-g6" is in the list.\n\nBut let\'s focus on Qh5 first.\n\nIf I play **Q: d1-h5**, and Black plays ...g6, then Qh4, and now I threaten Qxh8 or Qxg6+ if king moves.\n\nBut better: after Qh5, if ...g6, then **Ng5!** or **Nxg6!**?\n\nWait — e5 to g6 is a valid move: **N: e5-g6**.\n\nBut that’s a different plan.\n\nBack: Qh5 threatens Qxh7#.\n\nOnly legal replies: \n- ...g6\n- ...Qe7\n- ...Nh6 (awkward)\n- ...Kd8 (ugly)\n\nBest is ...g6, then Qh4.\n\nNow, I can consider **N: e5-g6!**, sacrificing knight: Nxg6 hxg6 Qxg6+ Kd8 (only) Qf6+ Ke8 Qxh8 — winning rook.\n\nBut is that sound?\n\nLet’s calculate:\n\n1. Qh5 g6  \n2. Qh4 Nxg6??  \n3. hxg6 hxg6  \n4. Qxg6+ Kd8  \n5. Qf6+ Ke8  \n6. Qxh8 — I’m up a rook.\n\nBut after 2. Qh4, Black can play ...Bg7, blocking future checks.\n\nBetter line:  \n1. Qh5 g6  \n2. Qh4 Bg7  \n3. Nxg6! hxg6  \n4. Qxg6+ Kd8  \n5. Qf6+ Ke8  \n6. Qxh8 — same thing.\n\nYes! Because after Nxg6, even if hxg6, Qxg6+ forces the king to d8 (can\'t go to f8 because of bishop on f1? Wait — bishop on f1 is mine, white. Black king on e8, after Qxg6+, king cannot go to f8 because of potential discovered check? No — no piece behind.\n\nKing can go to f8? Yes — from e8 to f8 is legal.\n\nAfter 4. Qxg6+ Kf8! — better than Ke7 or Kd8.\n\nThen 5. Qf6+ Kg8 — back to start.\n\nBut I have Qf6, threatening mate with Qg7# — supported by bishop on f1? No — bishop on f1 is mine, but it\'s on f1 — diagonal to g2, h3 — not helping on g7.\n\nWho controls g7? My queen on f6 attacks g7 only if supported.\n\nQf6 attacks g7 diagonally? Qf6 to g7 is adjacent — yes, queen covers g7.\n\nSo Qf6 + Kh7?? Qg7# — but Black won\'t allow.\n\nAfter Qf6, Black can play ...Rh6 to block or ...Be7.\n\nBut I also have **B: c1-g5** ready.\n\nSo plan:  \n1. Qh5 g6  \n2. Qh4 Bg7  \n3. Nxg6! hxg6  \n4. Qxg6+ Kf8  \n5. Qf6 Be7  \n6. Qg5! — threatening Qd8# or Qg7.\n\nVery strong.\n\nBut does Black have better than 1...g6?\n\nWhat if 1...Qe7? Then 2. Nxg6! hxg6 3. Qxh8 — winning rook immediately, since Qh8 is hanging.\n\nBecause queen on h8 is undefended.\n\nWait — is Rh8 defended? By whom? Black has knight on b8, bishop on f8, but no protection on h8.\n\nSo if I play Qh5, and Black plays ...Qe7, then **N: e5-g6!**\n\nhxg6? Then Qxh8 — I win a rook.\n\nOr ...fxg6? Then Qxe7+ Kxe7, and I win a piece (knight for bishop/pawn imbalance? But I had knight sacrifice).\n\nAfter Nxe7+ Kxe7, Qxh8 — still win rook.\n\nBut better: after N: e5-g6, if ...hxg6, Qxh8 — clean win of rook.\n\nIf ...fxg6, then Qxe7+ Kxe7, and I have Nxg6+ or something — but anyway, I\'m up material.\n\nBut simplest: if Black plays ...Qe7, I play Nxg6! hxg6 Qxh8 — win rook.\n\nIf Black plays ...g6, I play Qh4, and prepare Nxg6 or Bg5.\n\nSo **Q: d1-h5** initiates a devastating attack.\n\nIs it safe? My queen is exposed, but I have follow-up.\n\nOther options like Nc6 are slower.\n\nTherefore, best move is **Q: d1-h5**\n\nAnswer: Q: d1-h5'}
