fe? But is it attacked?\n     - White queen on f5 — does it attack d7? f5 to d7 is diagonal? f5-e6-d7 — yes, two squares down-left — so yes, queen attacks d7.\n   - e7 — occupied.\n   - f7 — attacked by queen.\n   - So only safe square is **d8**.\n\nAh — so after Ne7+, black king escapes to d8.\n\nThen no mate on f7.\n\nBut still — queen can go to f7, attacking f8, but king is on d8 — not in check.\n\nSo no immediate mate.\n\nBack to **Ne5** — still strong, threatens Qxf7+ next, which wins the exchange or more.\n\nBut better idea: **Q: f5-f7+** immediately?\n\nCheck: Qf5-f7+ — queen takes on f7 with check.\n\nBlack must respond:\n- Kxf7 — king takes\n- Then Nxe5+ — knight fork! Attacks king and queen (but black queen is missing?), so just gains a piece — knight checks, king must move, we win a pawn or more.\n\nAfter Kxf7, Ng5+ — knight from g6 to e5? Already did. Wait — if we play Qxf7+ first, then knight is still on g6.\n\nSo:\n1. Qxf7+ Kxf7\n2. Nxe5+ — fork: attacks king on f7 and what else? e5 is central.\n   - After Kf6? Or Ke6? King moves.\n   - Then Nxc6? But c6 has no black piece. Or Nd7? Not clear.\n\nBut we win a pawn (f7) and threaten more.\n\nBut better: keep queen active and develop initiative.\n\nAnother candidate: **B: c1-g5** — developing bishop, pinning a knight or preparing kingside attack.\n\nBut black has knight on b8, not developed.\n\nWait — best move: **N: g6-e5**\n\nWhy?\n- Attacks f7, which is only defended by the king.\n- Prepares Qxf7# if black ever moves the king.\n- Forces black to waste time addressing the threat.\n- Centralizes knight.\n- May lead to discovery attacks.\n\nAlso, **Q: f5-h5** — threatens Qxh7 if Bh6 or something, but h7 is defended.\n\nBut **N: g6-e5** seems strongest.\n\nWait — what about **Q: f5-f8+**? Queen to f8 — is that in the list?\n\nLooking at valid moves: **Q: f5-f8** — yes, it's listed.\n\nQf5-f8+ — check!\n\nKing cannot take (queen protected? by whom? nobody — but is it safe?\n\nf8 has BBF0 — black bishop on f