gal non-promotion move.\n\nBut wait — re-examining: the pawn on e5 is white (PWF1), so advancing to e6 is possible. However, more importantly: **the black king is on d5**, and my bishop on f4 is attacking e5 — but not directly threatening check. However, if I push **e5-e6**, I open up the diagonal for the bishop on d7 (light-squared bishop) to target the black king along the c8-h3 diagonal? Let\'s map:\n\n- Bishop on d7 (white) — can move along c8, b9… no, that\'s off-board. From d7, it controls c6, b5, a4 — and also e8, f5, g4, h3. So it does **not** directly attack d5.\n\nBut what about **f4-e3** or other moves?\n\nWait — let\'s reconsider: is there a tactic with **e5-e6**?\n\nIf I play **e5-e6**, I advance the pawn. But what does that do?\n\n- It opens the e-file slightly.\n- It gains space.\n- But more importantly: it threatens **e6-e7**, then e8=Q.\n\nBut black may not have time to respond.\n\nAlternatively, consider **Bf4-g5**, attacking something?\n\nWait — better idea: look at **d7-b5+**! That\'s in the valid move list: B: d7-b5\n\nDoes that give check?\n\nFrom d7 to b5: that\'s two left, two up — no, d7 to c6 is one left one up, b5 is two left two up — yes, same diagonal.\n\nBut is b5 on the diagonal from d7? d7–c6–b5 — yes, it\'s a diagonal. And from b5, does it attack the black king on d5?\n\nb5 to d5 is two files right — horizontal — not diagonal. So no check.\n\nWait — how about **Bf4-e5+**? Is that a valid move? Not in the list — Be5 isn\'t listed. Only B: f4-e3, d2, c1, g3, g5, h6.\n\nSo **Bf4-g5** is possible — but does that help?\n\nBg5 attacks h6, f4, e3, d2, c1 — and also h4, g5 — not particularly threatening.\n\nWait — back to **pawn e5-e6**: this move gains space and brings me closer to promotion. But more importantly: after e6, next move e7, then e8=Q. But black could capture on e6? Who captures?\n\nBlack has a piece on f7 — knight on e7? No — board shows: e7 is empty. Black has knight on e7? No — 7th rank: a7=P, d7=B (white!), e7=?, f7=?, g7=P