ok off the file, not useful; no clear target.\n- **R: h1-g1 / h1-f1**: Rook lift, but again slow; no immediate threat.\n- **K: e1-d1 / e1-d2 / e1-e2 / e1-f1 / e1-g1**: King moves — dangerous when we can punish Black’s exposed king. Also, moving the king now forfeits castling; not justified unless forced.\n- **B: f4-e3 / d2 / c1 / g3 / e5 / d6 / c7 / b8 / g5 / h6**: Most of these are passive or slow. But one stands out: **B: f4-c7** is not listed, but wait — actually **B: f4-c7** is NOT in the valid move list. Looking carefully: **B: f4-c7** is missing. But **B: f4-e5**, **d6**, **c7**? Wait — checking the list:  \n  Valid moves include: **B: f4-e5, B: f4-d6, B: f4-c7** — yes! **B: f4-c7** *is* present.\n\nWait — correction: In your provided valid moves list, you wrote:  \n"B: f4-e3, B: f4-d2, B: f4-c1, B: f4-g3, B: f4-e5, B: f4-d6, B: f4-c7, B: f4-b8, B: f4-g5, B: f4-h6"\n\nYes — **B: f4-c7** is a valid move.\n\nNow, **B: f4-c7** attacks the square c7, which is occupied by... nothing? Wait — c7 is empty? No — looking at the board:\n\nRow 7: a7=PBF0, b7=empty, c7=empty, d7=BWF1? Wait — no!\n\nWait — there\'s a critical confusion.\n\nYou wrote:\n\nRow 7: |PBF0|    |    |BWF1|NBF1|    |PBF0|    |\n\nSo:\na7: PBF0 (Black pawn)\nb7: empty\nc7: empty\nd7: BWF1 → White bishop? On d7? That can’t be — it\'s Black\'s side.\n\nBut earlier you said: "Current state representation", and d7 = BWF1?\n\nThat would mean a **White bishop on d7** — deep in enemy territory.\n\nIs that correct?\n\nYes — according to the board, d7 has **BWF1** — White bishop, moved, en passant flag irrelevant.\n\nSo White has a bishop on d7 (BWF1), not Black.\n\nThen the move **B: d7-c6** etc. are for that bishop.\n\nBut then **B: f4-...** refers to the other White bishop on f4.\n\nSo two White bishops: one on d7 (BWF1), one on f4 (BWF1).\n\nAnd Black has a bishop on f8? No — f8 is empty. b8=RBF1, g8=empty, h8=RBF1.\n\nBlack has a knight on b8? No — b8=NBF1? Wait — row 8:\n\na8: RBF0, b8: NBF0, c8: empty, d8: empty, e8: empty, f8: BBF0, g8: empty, h8: RBF1\n\nSo Black has knight on b8, bishop on f8, rooks on a8 and h8.\n\nBack to evaluation.\n\nWhite has a bishop on d7 (BWF1) — how did it get there? Possibly recently played. Now it can go to c6, b5, a4, c8, or e8.\n\nBut **B: d7-c6** threatens nothing directly.\n\nHowever, **B: f4-e5+**? Let\'s see — e5 is empty. If I play Bf4-e5, does it check the king?\n\nKing is on d5 — so e5 is adjacent diagonally — yes! From f4 to e5 is a diagonal move: f4 → e5 is one square up-left — yes, diagonal.\n\nSo **B: f4-e5** puts a direct attack on the Black king at d5 — **check!**\n\nMoreover, can the king capture? d5 to e5 — yes, but e5 would be occupied by the bishop. So if I play Be5+, Black king can take on e5? Only if it\'s safe.\n\nBut let’s analyze:\n\n**B: f4-e5+** — checks the Black king on d5.\n\nBlack king has limited escape squares:\n- c6? — is it attacked? Currently no known attacker, but White has bishop on d7 — d7 to c6 is possible, but not currently attacking c6.\n- c5? — off board.\n- d4? — possible.\n- e4? — possible.\n- e6? — possible.\n- d6? — possible.\n\nBut after Be5+, Black must respond.\n\nBut more importantly — is this winning?\n\nAlternatively, even better: **B: f4-d6+** — bishop from f4 to d6 — that’s two left, two up — diagonal? f4 → e5 → d6 — yes, diagonal. Is d6 protected? Currently no.\n\nd6 is adjacent to d5 — so **B: f4-d6** also gives **check**!\n\nSimilarly, **B: f4-c7**? c7 is three left, three up — f4 to c7: f4-e5-d6-c7 — yes, diagonal. But c7 is not near d5 — doesn\'t give check.\n\nBut **B: f4-e5** and **B: f4-d6** both give check.\n\nNow, **B: f4-d6+** is particularly strong because:\n- It comes with tempo.\n- After Bxd6+, Black must recapture or move king.\n- If Kxd6, then Qd1-d5+?? But I don’t have a queen out.\n- But I do have a bishop on d7 — B: d7-c6+! After Kxd6, then Bxc6+ would be check, but only if the king is on d6.\n\nWait: Suppose:\n1. Bf4-d6+ → Kx d6 (only way to stop check, since