ly attacks d5** — the Black king is safe for now, despite being exposed.\n\nBut why is it on d5? Likely a blunder, but not immediately losing.\n\nNow, what can I do?\n\nMy strongest idea: **B: d7-c6+**\n\nWhy? Bishop on d7 to c6 — that’s one left, one up — diagonal — yes.\n\nAfter **B: d7-c6+**, Black king on d5 is attacked — check!\n\nHow can Black respond?\n- Capture on c6? But no Black piece attacks c6 except possibly pawns — c7 is empty, d7 was just vacated. No Black piece on b7 or d7 to recapture. So if king captures Bxc6, then it\'s Kxc6, but White can recapture? With what? Knight? None nearby. Bishop on f4? Can it attack c6? f4 to c6: Δf=-3, Δr=+2 — not diagonal. No.\n\nSo after Bxc6+ Kxc6, no immediate follow-up.\n\nBut the point is: **it forces the king to move**.\n\nPossible escapes from d5 under attack from c6:\n- d4\n- e4\n- e5\n- e6\n- d6\n- c5 (off board)\n- c6 is the attacker, so not allowed.\n\nSo legal: d4, e4, e5, e6, d6.\n\nAll are possible.\n\nBut now, after Bc6+, if king moves to e5, then Bf4-d3 or something? Not strong.\n\nBut consider: **B: d7-b5+**\n\nSame idea — bishop to b5: d7 to b5 — two left, two up — diagonal — yes.\n\nAttacks d5? b5 to d5: two right, same rank — horizontal — no.\n\nSo not a check.\n\nOnly **B: d7-c6** gives check.\n\nOther moves like **B: d7-b5** or **a4** don’t attack the king.\n\nBut **B: d7-e8**? To e8 — one right, one up — diagonal — yes. Does it attack anything? e8 is near f8 bishop — maybe preparing tactics?\n\nBut not urgent.\n\nAnother idea: **P: a2-a4** — opens the a-file, prepares rook lift.\n\nOr **R: a1-b1** — same.\n\nBut the most forcing move is **B: d7-c6+**, which checks the king and disrupts Black’s coordination.\n\nAfter **B: d7-c6+**, suppose Kd4 — then I can play Bf4-e3+, another check! Forcing the king further into danger.\n\nFor example:\n1. B: d7-c6+ Kd4\n2. B: f4-e3+ Ke5 (or Kd3, but Kd3 allows Qd1-d5+? No queen)\nBut Bf4-e3+ attacks d4 and f4, etc.\n\nIf Kd4, then Be3+ forces Ke5 or Kc5 (invalid) or Kd3.\n\nIf Ke5, then Bg5+ or something.\n\nBut I don’t have a piece on g5.\n\nBut I do have **B: f4-g5** as a valid move.\n\nBut not immediately.\n\nAlternatively, after Kd4, I could play c2-c3 or something.\n\nBut the key is: **B: d7-c6+** is a strong developing move with tempo.\n\nOther promising move: **B: f4-e5** — although it doesn\'t check, it occupies the center and eyes g7 and h8.\n\nBut **B: d7-c6+** is more forcing.\n\nAlso, note that if Black ever plays ...Ke6, then Bf4-d2 or Bf4-c1 might pin something, but not clear.\n\nAnother candidate: **P: g2-g4** — prepares kingside expansion, but slow.\n\nBest move is the check: **B: d7-c6+**\n\nIt forces the king to move, gains initiative, and may lead to tactical opportunities.\n\nReason: The Black king is stranded in the center, and delivering checks now can disrupt their coordination, potentially leading to material gain or mating attacks. B: d7-c6+ is the only immediate check available, and it forces the king to a less safe square.\n\nAnswer: B: d7-c6'}
