  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |    |    |RBF1|    |    |BBF0|    |RBF1| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|    |    |    |NBF1|    |PBF0|    | 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |NBF1|    |    |    |PWF1|    |    |PBF1| 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |KBF1|    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |BWF1|    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|    |    |    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |    |    |    |RWF1|KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a3, B: a4-b3, B: a4-c2, B: a4-b5, B: a4-c6, B: a4-d7, B: a4-e8, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, R: d1-c1, R: d1-b1, R: d1-a1, R: d1-d2, R: d1-d3, R: d1-d4, R: d1-d5, R: d1-d6, R: d1-d7, R: d1-d8, K: e1-d2, K: e1-e2, K: e1-f1, K: e1-g1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king has not moved (KWF0), meaning castling is still a possibility. The black king on e5 is surprisingly advanced and exposed in the center, which is highly unusual and dangerous for Black—likely a blunder. Black’s king is currently on e5 with minimal protection. My light-squared bishop on a4 is actively placed and exerts pressure along the a4–e8 diagonal, which passes very close to the black king. Additionally, several of my pawns have solid development potential, and my rooks are connected on the back rank.\n\nBlack appears to have neglected king safety entirely—no nearby defenders, and their pieces are scattered. Notably, there is no black queen visible, suggesting it may have been captured or this is a handicap position, but regardless, the e5 king is vulnerable.\n\nEvaluation:\n- B: a4-e8: This move captures the knight on g6 if it were there, but actually, e8 is empty. However, a4-e8 puts the bishop directly attacking the square f7 and keeps pressure on the long diagonal. But more importantly—wait: the black king is on e5, and the bishop on a4 controls b5, c6, d7, e8. It does **not** currently attack e5 because it's two squares away diagonally (a4 to e5 is 4 right, 1 up — not diagonal). So no direct attack.\n  \n- B: a4-b5: Probes the center, threatens to exchange on c6 or support central control. But b5 is defended by black’s pawn on c6? Wait—there is no pawn on c6. Board shows c6 is empty. Black has a knight on c6? No—c6 is empty. Let's recheck:\n\nLooking again:\n- Black knight on c6? No: NBF1 is on e7 and c6 is empty. Actually, knight is on e7? No: NBF1 is on e7? Wait, board says:\n    - e7: NBF1 → yes, knight on e7\n    - c6: NBF1 → yes! a6: NBF1 → knight on a6? Wait, no:\n\nWait, correction:\n- Row 6: a6: NBF1 → Knight on a6\n- e6: PWF1 → white pawn on e6\n- h6: PBF1 → black pawn on h6\n\nSo black has knights on a6 and e7, rooks on c8 and h8, bishop on f8, king on e5.\n\nNow, bishop on a4 (BWF1) can go to:\n- B: a4-c6 — this lands on c6, which is empty. c6 is two right, two up from a4 — that’s diagonal, so valid. And c6 is only two squares from e5 (diagonally: c6 to d5 to e5, but not aligned). c6 to e5 is two right, one down — not diagonal. So no direct attack.\n\nBut crucially: **Does any piece attack e5?**\n- Bishop a4 → controls b5, c6, d7, e8 — none of which is e5.\n- Rook on d1: controls d-file — d5 is on its path. d5 is adjacent to e5. But not attacking e5.\n- Can we play something that attacks e5?\n\nWait — **knight on b1?** No, no knight listed on b1. Our pieces: \n- Pawns: a2, b2, f2, g2, h2\n- Bishop: a4\n- Rooks: d1, h1\n- King: e1\n\nNo knight. So no immediate fork or attack.\n\nBut wait — **if we play B: a4-d7**, that goes to d7, which is one diagonal step from e5 (d7 to e5 is one right, two down — not diagonal). No.\n\nBut **B: a4-c2** — retreats, bad.\n\nWait — **is there a discovered attack possible?**\n\nAlternatively, let’s consider **central control and development**.\n\nWhite has a pawn on e6 (PWF1), which is deep in enemy territory. It restricts black’s space.\n\nBut the key idea: **the black king is extremely exposed**. We should look to open lines.\n\nBest plan: **Develop with tempo, threaten the king, prepare for attack**.\n\nOne strong candidate: **b2-b4** or **f2-f4** to push pawns and gain space.\n\nBut even better: **B: a4-b5+**? Does b5 check the king?\n\nFrom a4 to b5: one right, one up — yes, diagonal. b5 to e5 is three right — same rank? b5 to e5 is three files over, same rank? No: b5 is rank 5, e5 is rank 5 — yes! So b5 is on rank 5.\n\nSo if bishop moves to b5, it would be on b5, and the black king is on e5 — same rank. But bishop moves diagonally, not horizontally. So **bishop on b5 does NOT attack e5** — they are on same rank, but bishop can't move horizontally.\n\nOnly rook or queen could attack along the rank.\n\nSo no check.\n\nBut **b5 is a strong outpost**: it pressures c6, may force ...c6-c5 or ...b7-b6, and potentially supports a future knight jump or pawn break.\n\nBut we don’t have a knight.\n\nAlternatively, **f2-f4** opens the diagonal for the bishop and gains space. It also prepares kingside expansion.\n\nBut best idea: **develop while threatening**.\n\nAnother thought: **can we castle to safety?** Yes — king hasn’t moved, h1 rook hasn’t moved, no pieces between e1 and h1 except f1 and g1 — both empty. So castling kingside is legal.\n\nCastling: **K: e1-g1** is in the valid move list.\n\nThat’s **King to g1**, which castles kingside.\n\nThis is attractive: it gets the king to safety, connects the rooks, and activates the h1 rook.\n\nCurrently, the king is in the center, and although the position is closed, pushing pawns like b2-b4 or f2-f4 might open the center.\n\nGiven that Black’s king is in the center, **both kings are at risk if the center opens**. So **castling first** is prudent before engaging in aggression.\n\nMoreover, after castling, we can consider f4, h4, or doubling rooks on the d-file.\n\nOther moves like **R: d1-d7**? Not available — d7 is occupied? d7 is empty. But R: d1-d7 is not in the list. Valid rook moves go to d8, d6, etc., but not d7? Wait — list includes: R: d1-d7 — yes! It is in the list.\n\nR: d1-d7 — rook to d7.\n\nWhat's on d7? Nothing — empty. But black has a pawn on d7? No — looking at board: d7 is empty. c8 has rook, d8 empty, e8 empty, f8 has bishop, so d7 is empty.\n\nBut behind it: black king on e5 — so rook on d7 doesn't attack anything directly.\n\nBut it **controls the 7th rank**, pressures pawns, and supports an attack.\n\nHowever, **castling feels more urgent** — king safety first.\n\nAlso, **B: a4-e8** — bishop to e8 — is that good? e8 is where the black king started. It’s far from action. Retreats the bishop.\n\nNo — bad.\n\nBest options:\n1. **K: e1-g1** — castle kingside: king safety, activates rook.\n2. **f2-f4** — gains space, opens diagonal, prepares for attack.\n3. **R: d1-d7** — occupies 7th rank, annoying for Black.\n4. **B: a4-b5** — outpost, pressures queenside.\n\nBut **after castling, we can do all the others safely**.\n\nWithout castling, pushing pawns risks counterattack.\n\nFor example, if we play f2-f4, then e5-g4? No — king can't move like that.\n\nBut Black might play ...Nxe6 or ...Ng6, increasing pressure.\n\nStill, our king is more secure than Black’s.\n\nBut principle: **when opponent’s king is exposed, prioritize your own safety so you can launch an attack without fear**.\n\nSo **castling is key**.\n\nMoreover, castling is in the move list: **K: e1-g1**\n\nIt’s legal: king hasn’t moved (KWF0), rook on h1 hasn’t moved (RWF0), no pieces between, and the king is not in check (no threats to e1), and the squares f1 and g1 are not under attack.\n\nAre they?\n\nIs e1 attacked? No obvious piece attacks e1.\n\nBlack has:\n- Rook on c8 — controls c-file, not e1.\n- Rook on h8 — h-file, not e1.\n- Bishop on f8 — diagonal: f8-a3? f8 to a3 is 5 left, 5 down — a3 exists. But a3 is empty. Does it hit e4? f8 to e7 to d6 to c5 to b4 to a3 — so yes, the a3-c5 diagonal. e1 is not on that.\n\ne1 is on e1 — bishop on f8 controls dark squares, e1 is light square — no.\n\nKnights on a6 and e7 — a6 knight: can it attack c5? c5 is two right, one up — yes, but not e1. e1 is four right, five down — too far.\n\ne7 knight: e7 to f5, d5, g6, c6 — not e1.\n\nSo e1, f1, g1 are safe.\n\nThus, **castling is legal and safe**.\n\nTherefore, **K: e1-g1** is excellent.\n\nReason: Castling improves king safety, activates the rook, and prepares for a coordinated attack. Given that Black’s king is dangerously exposed on e5, securing my own king allows me to launch a pawn storm or piece attack without risk. It’s a foundational move before engaging in tactical operations.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1"}
