\n1 |    |    |    |RWF1|    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a3, B: a4-b3, B: a4-c2, B: a4-b5, B: a4-c6, B: a4-d7, B: a4-e8, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, R: d1-c1, R: d1-b1, R: d1-a1, R: d1-d2, R: d1-d3, R: d1-d4, R: d1-d5, R: d1-e1, P: e6-e7, R: f1-e1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a semi-open position. My king is on g1 (KWF1), still unmoved and not in immediate danger. The black king is on e5 (KBF1), which is unusually far advanced and looks highly exposed—this is a critical vulnerability. Black’s king has already moved but is dangerously centralized without sufficient cover. Black has a bishop on f6 (BBF0), rooks on c8 and h8, and knights on a6 and d5. My light-squared bishop on a4 (BWF1) is actively placed, eyeing the long diagonal toward the black king. I also have pawns ready to advance, particularly on b2, f2, g2, and h2.\n\nBlack\'s position has serious weaknesses, especially around the king on e5. It is currently on a dark square, and my bishop on a4 controls key light squares, but other pieces can exploit this exposure. Notably, there is no black piece defending the e5 square directly, and the king lacks pawn cover.\n\nEvaluation:\n- B: a4-b5 – Attacks c6, pressures knight on d5 indirectly, but doesn’t immediately threaten mate.\n- B: a4-c6 – Very strong! This pins the knight on d5 if it were to move, but more importantly, it attacks the black king directly. c6 is a dark square, and with the king on e5, this creates a discovered attack potential. Also, after Bc6+, Black must respond: either block or move the king. But crucially, **Bc6+ is check**, since the bishop attacks e5 diagonally (a4–c6–e5 is a diagonal). This is a discovered-check candidate if any piece moves out of the way, but here it\'s direct!\n  - Wait: a4 to c6 is along the a4–e8 diagonal? Let\'s verify: a4 (a-file, 4th rank), c6 (c-file, 6th rank): difference is +2 files, +2 ranks — yes, that\'s a diagonal move. And from c6 to e5 is -2 files, -1 rank — not aligned. Wait, no: c6 to e5 is +2 files, -1 rank — not diagonal. So **bishop on c6 does NOT attack e5**. Diagonals from c6 go to a8, b5, d7, e8, etc., but not e5. e5 is two files right and one rank down — not diagonal. So **Bc6 does NOT give check**. So that idea fails.\n\nLet’s reassess:\n- a4-e8: That’s a full diagonal. a4→b5→c6→d7→e8. Yes, e8 is on the same diagonal. But e8 is empty. However, **does a4-e8 attack anything valuable?** It points at e8, but more importantly, does it threaten anything along the way?\n\nBut wait — what about **a4-d7**? That\'s on the list. d7 is occupied by a black pawn (PBF0)? No — looking at the board: d7 is empty. c7 is empty, d7 is empty. Black has pawns on a7, g7, h7. So d7 is free. But again, not obviously threatening.\n\nWait — let\'s reconsider **B: a4-b3** — moving to b3. From b3, the bishop eyes the c4, d5, e6, f7, g8 diagonal. In particular, **f7** is a classic weak point — only defended by the black king on e5! And f7 is currently undefended except by the king. If I could attack f7 with force, that might be decisive.\n\nCurrently, **Qh5#** would be mate, but I don\'t have a queen. But perhaps a discovered attack?\n\nWait — I don\'t have a queen developed yet. But look: if I play **B: a4-e8**, that places the bishop on e8 — which is behind black’s back rank. But e8 is attacked by nothing, and black has a rook on c8 and h8 — so maybe not useful.\n\nAlternative idea: **P: e6-e7** — this pawn moves to e7, attacking e8 and potentially promoting next move if supported. But e7 is on the same file as the black king on e5 — so this pawn advances toward queening. After e7, if not captured, e8=Q next move. Is it safe?\n\nWho defends e7? Currently, no white piece does. Black can capture en passant? No — e6-e7 is only one square. Wait — valid move is P: e6-e7 — yes, from e6 to e7. But e6 has a white pawn (PWF1), so it has already moved — so e6-e7 is a legal single push.\n\nAfter e6-e7, the pawn is on e7, one step from promotion. Can black stop it? They can play ...Nxe7, since knight on d5 can take on e7. So unless I defend it, it will be captured.\n\nDo I want to lose a pawn for free? Only if it opens up tactics.\n\nBut consider: after e6-e7, if black plays Nxd5? No, Nxe7. Then recapture? I don’t have a piece attacking e7 except possibly discovered.\n\nAlternatively, **P: b2-b4** or **f2-f4** — gaining space, but not urgent.\n\nWait — back to the bishop: **B: a4-b5+** — move to b5. Is that check? From b5, does it attack e5? b5 to e5 is three files right, same rank — horizontal, not diagonal. Diagonally, b5 attacks a4, c6, d7, e8 — not e5. e5 is two files right and one rank down — not aligned. So no.\n\nBut **B: a4-c2** — c2 is on my second rank. That retreats the bishop — seems passive.\n\nWait — what about **R: d1-d5**? Is that possible? d5 is occupied by a black knight (NBF1). So R: d1-d5 means capturing the knight on d5.\n\nYes! **R: d1-d5** is on the valid move list. That’s a rook lift from d1 to d5, capturing the knight on d5.\n\nThat seems extremely strong.\n\nWhy?\n- Black king is on e5 — very exposed.\n- Capturing the knight on d5 with the rook attacks the king directly — **Rd5 gives check!**\n- From d5 to e5 is one square right — horizontal. Yes! So **Rd5+** is check.\n- Now, how can black escape? \n    - King moves: e5-f5, e5-e4, e5-e6, e5-d6, e5-d4? But d5 is now occupied by white rook.\n    - But is f5 available? f5 is empty. e4? e4 is empty. e6? e6 wa