\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, B: b5-a4, B: b5-c4, B: b5-d3, B: b5-e2, B: b5-f1, B: b5-a6, B: b5-c6, B: b5-d7, P: c3-c4, R: e8-d8, R: e8-c8, R: e8-b8, R: e8-a8, R: e8-e7, R: e8-e6, R: e8-e5, R: e8-e4, R: e8-e3, R: e8-e2, R: e8-e1, R: e8-f8, P: f2-f4, P: f2-g3, P: f2-f3, K: g1-f1, K: g1-h1, R: g8-f8, R: g8-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a relatively open position with several pieces developed. My king is on g1 (KWF1), having already moved, so castling is not possible. There are two rooks on e8 and g8 (both RWF1), indicating they haven\'t moved yet. The black king is on c7 (KBF1), which is unusually exposed and far from safety—this could be a tactical opportunity. Black has a bishop on b5 (BWF1) — wait, that must be an error in labeling; actually, looking at the encoding: BWF1 means *White* Bishop on b5? But b5 has a piece labeled BWF1 — that would mean it\'s a **White** bishop on b5, but earlier we said "You are playing as White", so that checks out — yes, we have a White bishop on b5.\n\nWait — correction: Looking back:\n\nAt b5: BWF1 → White Bishop, never moved? But bishops start on c1/f1 — this must be a captured or promoted piece? Or perhaps it\'s just developed there. Wait no — b5 is occupied by BWF1 → White bishop. That’s fine — likely developed there.\n\nBut also: pawns on f2, g2, a2 — all PWF0 → unmoved.\n\nBlack pawns on f7, g7, a5, h5 — some moved.\n\nKey observation: Black king is on c7 — very unsafe! Centralized and exposed. It\'s on the 7th rank, surrounded by little protection. We have a White bishop on b5 — attacking c6, d7, etc. From b5, it eyes the diagonal toward the black king.\n\nMoreover, if we play Bb5-a6, we attack the black king directly — is that check?\n\nLet’s calculate: Ba6 would place the bishop on a6, which is on the same diagonal as c7 — yes: a6 to c7 is a diagonal (a6-b7-c8? No — wrong direction). a6 to c7: a6-b6-c7? That’s not diagonal. Diagonals from a6 go up-right: b7, c8 — nowhere near c7. So Ba6 does **not** attack c7.\n\nFrom b5, the bishop attacks along four diagonals:\n\n- b5 → a6 (no threat)\n\n- b5 → c6, d7, e8 — d7 and e8 are empty and then rook on e8\n\n- b5 → c4, d3, e2, f1 — f1 is empty\n\n- b5 → a4 — a4 is empty\n\nSo currently, no direct attack on black king.\n\nBut what about **Bb5 × c7**? Is that possible? c7 has KBF1 — black king. Can we capture it? Only if it\'s not protected.\n\nIs c7 protected? Let\'s see what defends c7.\n\nPieces near c7: black pawn on f7? No. Own pieces: only king. What about other black pieces? We don’t see any defenders around — no rook on c8, no queen, no bishop. So if we can move to c7, and it\'s unprotected, then Bxc7 would win the king — i.e., deliver checkmate?\n\nWait — can you capture the king? In standard chess, no — but capturing into check is illegal. However, if the king is under attack and cannot escape, it\'s checkmate.\n\nSo question: **Can White play Bb5×c7+?**\n\nIs c7 attacked by Bb5? Yes — b5 to c7 is one square up-right — that\'s a diagonal move of one square — yes, adjacent diagonally.\n\nSo **bishop on b5 attacks c7** — which has the black king.\n\nTherefore, **the black king is currently in check from the White bishop on b5**!\n\nBut why hasn\'t Black moved yet? This is **your turn**, White — meaning Black just played and now it\'s your move.\n\nBut if Black ended their turn with their king in check, that would be illegal.\n\nTherefore, this suggests that either:\n\n1. The position is invalid (but we assume it\'s legal), or\n\n2. The bishop on b5 is **not** delivering check — maybe it\'s blocked?\n\nWait — between b5 and c7: b5 → c6 → c7? No — diagonal is b5 → c6 → d7 → e8 or b5 → c4...\n\nWait — from b5 to c7: that’s b5 → c6 → d7 → e8 — no, c7 is not on that line.\n\nLet’s do coordinates:\n\n- b5 = file b (2), rank 5\n\n- c7 = file c (3), rank 7\n\nDifference: file +1, rank +2 → not equal → not diagonal.\n\nAh! **Not diagonal!** So bishop on b5 does **not** attack c7.\n\nDistance: Δfile = +1, Δrank = +2 — that’s a knight move.\n\nSo **no**, the bishop does not attack c7.\n\nThe squares the bishop on b5 attacks diagonally:\n\n- Up-right: c6 (f+1,r+1), d7 (f+2,r+2), e8 (f+3,r+3)\n\n- Down-right: c4 (f+1,r-1), d3 (r-2), e2, f1\n\n- Up-left: a6 (f-1,r+1)\n\n- Down-left: a4 (f-1,r-1)\n\nSo d7 is attacked — d7 is empty.\n\ne8 has a rook.\n\nNow, black king is on c7 — what attacks c7?\n\nOnly if a piece can reach it via knight move or diagonal or straight.\n\nBut no white piece currently attacks c7.\n\nNow, let\'s evaluate promising moves.\n\nLooking at valid moves:\n\nWe have:\n\n- Pawns: a2-a3/a4, c3-c4, f2-f3/f4/g3, g2-g3 (P: f2-g3), etc.\n\n- Bishop: b5 to a4/c4/d3/e2/f1/a6/c6/d7\n\n- Rooks: e8 and g8 can move\n\n- King: g1 to f1/h1\n\n- Also, P: f2-g3 — that’s a capture? g3 is empty.\n\nWait — current board:\n\ng7 has PWF1 — white pawn? No: g7 has PWF1? But that’s rank 7, file g — should be black side.\n\nWait — look again:\n\nRow 7:\n|    |    |KBF1|    |    |    |PWF1|    |\n\nPWF1 on g7 — that’s a **White** pawn on g7? That can\'t be right — unless it promoted or advanced.\n\nBut pawns promote on 8th rank — g7 is 7th — so possible: white pawn on g7, one step from promotion.\n\nYes — plausible.\n\nSimilarly, black pawns on a5 (PBF1), h5 (PBF1), f7 (PBF1)? Wait — f7: row 7, f-file — is empty. Row 7: a:empty, b:empty, c:KBF1, d:empty, e:empty, f:empty, g:PWF1, h:empty\n\nSo white pawn on g7 — very advanced.\n\nBlack pawns: a5 (PBF1), c3 h