ame — not diagonal. b5 to c6 is diagonal. So c5 is not under direct fire — but could be captured next move?\n\nBut more importantly: after Re8-e5+, suppose Black plays Kd5-c6? Then I play Bb5-d7+ or Bd7#? Let\'s see.\n\nBetter: after Re8-e5+, if Kd5-c6, then Bb5-a4+ forking king and possibly cutting off escape.\n\nBut even better: is there a faster mate?\n\nWhat about immediate threats?\n\nAnother idea: can I play Bb5-d7+ immediately? From b5 to d7: b→c→d (+2), 5→6→7 (+2) — yes, diagonal. Is d7 attacked? Yes. Is it occupied? Board shows d7 empty — row 7: only g7 has PWF1. So d7 is free.\n\nSo Bb5-d7+: checks the king on d5? d7 to d5 is vertical — same file, two squares down. So yes — rook-style, not bishop. Bishop on d7 attacks along diagonals — so from d7, diagonals are c6, b5, a4 / e6, f5, g4, h3 / and c8, b7, a6 / e8, f7, g6, h5.\n\nDoes it attack d5? Only if same diagonal — d7 to d5 is straight down — not diagonal. So no.\n\nSo Bb5-d7 does **not** check the king on d5.\n\nWhat about Bb5-c6+? b5 to c6: +1 file, +1 rank — diagonal — yes. c6 is empty? Row 6 is all empty — yes. So Bb5-c6+ checks the king on d5? c6 to d5 is +1 file, -1 rank — diagonal — yes! So bishop takes c6 with check.\n\nAnd after Bc6+, king must move — possible squares: c5, d6, e6, e4, c4, d4 (but d4 not labeled).\n\nd6: is it attacked? By bishop on c6 — c6 to d6 is horizontal — no. So d6 is safe? But rook on e8 can go to e6 — but not immediately.\n\nBut after Bc6+, if Kd5-d6, then Re8-e6+! Delivers check — and now king has no good escape: d5 is blocked, e6 attacked, f6? Not adjacent. From d6, king can go to c5, d5, e5, c6 (occupied), e7, d7, c7.\n\nBut Re8-e6+ attacks d6 — so king must leave.\n\nBut more importantly: is Bb5-c6+ legal? Yes — it\'s in the valid moves: "B: b5-c6".\n\nSimilarly, Re8-e5+ is also available.\n\nNow, evaluate key options:\n\n1. **Re8-e5+**: Attacks e5, checks king. After Kd5-c6 or Kd5-d6 or Kd5-e6, I can follow up with Bb5-a4+ (attacking c6) or Bb5-d7, etc. But e5 may 