lt for Black to escape — possible responses: Kb6, Kb5, Ke5, Kd5, etc., but many are unsafe.\n\nAlso, after Bc6+, we may follow up with rook lifts or even discovered attacks.\n\nOther options:\n- R: e5-e8 — also threatens the back rank, but black can easily move the king or interpose.\n- But **B: b5-c6+** is a forcing move — checks the king immediately — much stronger.\n\nWhy isn\'t B: b5-c6 marked with "+"? Because the move list doesn’t include notation — just src-dst.\n\nBut functionally, it is a check.\n\nAdditionally, this move gains tempo and may win material or lead to mate.\n\nCompare to:\n- R: e5-e8 — also strong, but less forcing than a direct check.\n- B: b5-c6+ is superior because it attacks the king directly.\n\nAlso, the bishop is currently on b5 — has it moved before? BWF1 — W=White, F=did NOT move 2 steps (irrelevant for bishop), 1 = has moved — yes, so no issue.\n\nNo obstruction — b5 to c6 is one square diagonally — valid bishop move.\n\nThus, **B: b5-c6** is a powerful, forcing check.\n\nReason: Delivering check to the exposed black king on d6 maximizes pressure and limits Black’s options. It gains initiative, potentially wins material, or leads to mating attacks. Among all valid moves, this is the most forcing and strategically dominant.\n\nAnswer: B: b5-c6'}
