\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: c3-c4, B: e8-d7, B: e8-c6, B: e8-b5, B: e8-a4, B: e8-f7, B: e8-g6, B: e8-h5, K: f2-e1, K: f2-e2, K: f2-e3, K: f2-f1, K: f2-f3, K: f2-g1, K: f2-g3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: g8-f8, R: g8-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a relatively open position with limited material. My king on f2 is safe for now, but there is potential danger if Black's bishop on e8 becomes active. Black has a rook on g8 and pawns on a5, f5, and h5, while I have pawns on a2, c3, g2, and two central pawns already advanced (g7-pawn captured?). My own rook is on g8? Wait — no, that must be a typo in interpretation: looking again, **RWF1** is on **g8**, which means *my* rook (White Rook) is still on g8, undeveloped. The Black king is on f5 — an unusually exposed position! That’s critical.\n\nWait — let’s re-parse the board carefully:\n\n- **e8**: BWF1 → White Bishop on e8? That can't be right because White’s bishops start on c1 and f1. This must be a **Black** Bishop. There's likely a mislabeling in your encoding.\n\nHold on — your piece encoding says `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`. So:\n\n- `BWF1`: Type=B, Color=W → White Bishop\n- But it's on e8 — which is deep in Black's territory. That suggests either a promotion or a massive blunder.\n\nBut wait — look at the pawns:\n\n- On g7: PWF1 → White pawn on g7, already moved.\n- On a2: PWF0 → White pawn, unmoved.\n- On c3: PWF1 → White pawn, moved.\n- On g2: PWF0 → White pawn, unmoved.\n- Black pawns: a5, f5, h5 — all PBF1 → moved, not en passant eligible.\n\nAlso:\n- KWF1: White King on f2\n- KBF1: Black King on f5 — extremely advanced and exposed!\n\nAnd:\n- RWF1 on g8 → White Rook on g8 — so it hasn't moved yet.\n- BWF1 on e8 → White Bishop on e8 — this is possible if it was developed via d3-e4-g6-h7-e8 or similar.\n\nSo despite being unusual, we accept the state as given: **White has a bishop on e8**, **rook on g8**, **king on f2**, and **Black king on f5**, dangerously far from safety.\n\nThis is key: **Black's king is on f5**, almost in the center, and I have a **bishop on e8** — one diagonal away from targeting the king.\n\nDiagonals from e8: e8-h5 is a diagonal. f5 is on the same diagonal? Let's see:\n\n- From e8 to f7, g6, h5 — yes, descending a1-h8 diagonal.\n- f5 is **not** on that diagonal. e8 to f5 is off-diagonal.\n\nBut wait: e8 to f5 is one rank down and one file right — that's just one square diagonally: **e8 attacks f7**, not f5.\n\nSo bishop on e8 does **not** attack f5.\n\nBut what about other pieces?\n\nI have a **rook on g8**, which can go to g6, attacking the black king on f5? No — g8 to g6 is vertical, not horizontal.\n\nRook on g8 can move along rank 8 or file g.\n\nFrom g8, moving down file g: g7 (occupied by my own pawn), so blocked.\n\nSo rook cannot act immediately.\n\nMy king is on f2 — safe.\n\nBlack king on f5 is exposed, but not currently under direct threat.\n\nNow, evaluating **valid moves**:\n\nAvailable moves:\n- Pawns: a2-a4, a2-a3, c3-c4, g2-g3, g2-g4\n- Bishop: e8-d7, e8-c6, e8-b5, e8-a4, e8-f7, e8-g6, e8-h5\n- King: f2-e1, f2-e2, f2-e3, f2-f1, f2-f3, f2-g1, f2-g3\n- Rook: g8-f8, g8-h8\n\nLet’s assess threats and opportunities.\n\n### Key Observations:\n1. **Black king on f5 is very exposed.** If I can bring pressure, maybe even deliver checkmate.\n2. My **bishop on e8** is well-placed for potential battery or discovered attack.\n3. Moving the bishop away could enable a discovered check from the rook on g8 if aligned.\n   - For example, if bishop moves off the g-file or 8th rank, rook on g8 can’t give discovered check unless king is on g-file.\n   - But king is on f5 — not on g-file or 8th rank.\n   - So discovered checks are unlikely.\n4. Can I threaten the black king?\n\nLook at **g2-g4**: this pawn move opens the g-file for the rook. Currently, g7 has a white pawn (PWF1), so g2-g4 would support a push or prepare kingside play.\n\nBut more importantly: **g2-g4** might be preparing to challenge the black king’s position.\n\nBut better idea: **king move f2-f3** or **f2-g3** brings my king closer to center, but is it safe?\n\nBlack has a bishop on e8? No — wait: **BWF1 on e8** is **White** bishop. Who is Black’s bishop? Not visible. Maybe captured.\n\nBlack’s only pieces seen: king f5, pawn a5, f5, h5, rook g8? No — **RWF1** is White rook on g8.\n\nWait — who owns the rook on g8? RWF1 → White. So Black’s rook must be elsewhere or captured.\n\nPossibly Black has lost material.\n\nGiven that, perhaps White is winning and should consolidate.\n\nBut let’s look at **tactical shots**.\n\nWhat if I play **Bishop e8-h5+**? That moves Bh5, attacking the black king on f5?\n\ne8 to h5: that's three files right, three ranks down — yes, diagonal. e8-f7-g6-h5. So h5 is on the same diagonal? e8 to h5 is a diagonal, but f5 is **not** on that line.\n\nThe diagonal from e8 is a1-h8 type: e8, f7, g6, h5.\n\nf5 is one rank below g6 — not on that diagonal.\n\nSo **e8-h5 does not attack f5**.\n\nSimilarly, e8-g6 attacks g6 — not f5.\n\nCan any piece attack f5?\n\nCurrent attackers: none.\n\nBut what if I play **King f2-f3**? Then I free up g2-g4, and centralize.\n\nOr **g2-g4** directly?\n\ng2-g4: advances pawn, opens possibility of g4-g5+, kicking the black king?\n\nBlack king on f5 — if I play g4-g5+, then king is attacked on f5 from g5 — yes, pawn on g5 attacks f6 and h6, not f5.\n\nPawn on g5 attacks f6 and h6. f5 is adjacent, but not attacked by g5.\n\nSo g4 doesn't directly threaten.\n\nBut **pawn on g7** — PWF1 — can it promote? g7 to g8 is one step — why hasn’t it moved?\n\nBecause it’s on g7, and g8 is occupied by **my own rook**! RWF1 on g8 blocks my pawn on g7.\n\nAh! Critical point: **I have a pawn on g7 and a rook on g8** — so the pawn cannot advance unless the rook moves.\n\nTherefore, **moving the rook from g8 is essential to allow the g7 pawn to promote**.\n\nThe pawn on g7 is one square from queening. If I can clear g8, then next move g7-g8=Q wins easily.\n\nSo **priority: move the rook from g8**.\n\nValid rook moves: **R: g8-f8, R: g8-h8**\n\nBoth vacate g8.\n\nAfter that, next turn I play g7-g8=Q, promoting to queen, which is crushing.\n\nSo the best plan is: **move rook away, then promote**.\n\nNow, which rook move is better?\n\n- **g8-f8**: puts rook on f8. f-file: f7 has nothing, f6 empty, f5 has black king. So rook on f8 attacks the king along the file? Only if no pieces block.\n\nFrom f8 to f5: passes through f7, f6. Are they empty?\n\nYes — f7: empty (no piece listed), f6: empty.\n\nSo **rook on f8 attacks the black king on f5** — delivering check!\n\nThat’s huge.\n\nIf I play **R: g8-f8**, then Black is in check on f5.\n\nBlack must respond: move king, block, or capture.\n\nPossible escapes:\n- f5-e5, f5-e6, f5-g5, f5-g6, f5-h5, f5-h6, etc., but also captures?\n\nCan he capture on f8? Only if he has a piece on f8 — no.\n\nOr block? He could play ...g7-g6? But he has no g7 pawn — his pawns are a5, f5, h5. f5 is the king.\n\nHe has no piece on g7 — g7 has **my** pawn (PWF1).\n\nSo Black has no way to block the rook check on f8.\n\nTherefore, **if I play R: g8-f8, it gives check**, and Black must move his king.\n\nAfter that, I can follow up with g7-g8=Q, promoting with check or mating.\n\nEven better: after Rf8+, suppose Black plays Kf5-e5 (or any escape), then g7-g8=Q is unstoppable.\n\nThen I have a queen and rook vs king — easy win.\n\nAlternatively, if I play **g8-h8**, rook goes to h8 — no check, less effective.\n\nSo **R: g8-f8** is superior — it’s a check and forces the black king to move, losing tempi.\n\nAdditionally, after the rook move, my g7 pawn is still protected? By whom?\n\ng7 pawn: currently supported? Not directly, but after rook moves, it can promote next move regardless.\n\nNo risk — I don’t need to protect it; I just need to move it next.\n\nSo **R: g8-f8** is a strong, forcing move.\n\nAre there any drawbacks?\n\nCould Black capture the rook on f8 later? Unlikely, and irrelevant — I’ll promote anyway.\n\nIs there any immediate counterplay? Doubtful — Black is in chaos with king on f5.\n\nOther moves like pushing pawns are slower.\n\nFor example, a2-a4 or g2-g4 delays the kill.\n\nEven king moves are passive.\n\nBishop moves: e8-h5? Might be useful, but not as urgent as forcing through promotion.\n\nMoreover, **R: g8-f8** is both developmental and aggressive.\n\nTherefore, best move: **R: g8-f8**\n\nAnswer: R: g8-f8"}
